<article class="env-remark" id="033O">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/033O">Remark <span data-tag="033O">28.7.8</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a normal scheme. If $X$ is locally Noetherian then we see that $X$ is integral if and only if $X$ is connected, see Lemma <a href="/tag/033N" data-tag="033N">28.7.7</a>. But there exists a connected affine scheme $X$ such that $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is a domain for all $x \in X$, but $X$ is not irreducible, see Examples, Section <a href="/tag/0568" data-tag="0568">110.6</a>. This example is even a normal scheme (proof omitted), so beware! </p>
</article>